{
  "name": "Foxfire",
  "notes": "Bioluminescence: things that glow where they are born and never travel. `speedVary` at 0.03 and no gravity, so the particles hang; `fade` is 0, because fade only fades OUT and a foxfire has to glow UP as well. The envelope lives in the COLOUR instead -- the index is age and the palette runs dark green through chartreuse to white-hot jade and back down to violet, so a particle's whole life is one traverse of it. `wrap` stays off: this is a gradient with two ends and both of them are dim, and joining them would mean the fade never completes.\n\n`rate` is on a `perlin` LFO rather than a sine, so the field clusters and thins the way a real one does instead of breathing on a metronome. The chain doubles that -- a second, slower perlin drives the FIRST one's rate, so the clustering itself has slow seasons: a minute where the bursts come thick, then a minute of near-silence.\n\nThe second layer is the mycelium under it, a very coarse noise at a fifth the opacity, drifting the other way. Its `indexBias` is on the same chain as the particle rate, so the ground glows brightest exactly where the population is densest -- cause and effect, from one source into two places. Poison scheme: three yellow-greens and jades inside 75 degrees with one violet 200 away, and the violet is only ever the last half-second of a particle's life.",
